  "summary": "I reviewed Harold Vance's active medications against RBC-PHARM-07 and made two changes. Metformin stays the same.\n\n**Values used:**\n- **Creatinine:** 1.21 mg/dL on 10/20. I didn't use the 1.88 from 10/21 because it was marked entered in error (mislabelled specimen from another patient).\n- **Weight:** 64.9 kg (143 lb), measured 10/20. I didn't use the 132 lb from 10/22 because it was his own estimate by phone, not a weighing.\n- **Age:** 82.\n\nThe pharmacy screen that triggered this used the 1.88 creatinine, so its recommendations don't hold. I redid the review with the valid values.\n\n**Results:**\n1. **Apixaban: increased to 5 mg twice daily** (new order mr-t0001, replaces the 2.5 mg order). He now meets only one of the three criteria for the lower dose (age 80 or older). His weight is over 60 kg and his creatinine is under 1.5. The 2.5 mg dose was set at his February discharge, when he weighed 57 kg and his creatinine was 1.71 during the kidney injury. Both have recovered.\n2. **Metformin: no change**, stays at 1000 mg twice daily. His eGFR is about 60, and the policy only calls for a change below 45.\n3. **Gabapentin: reduced from 600 mg three times daily to 600 mg twice daily** (new order mr-t0002, same 600 mg tablet). His creatinine clearance is about 43, which allows 400–1400 mg/day given twice daily, and he was on 1800 mg/day. I chose 1200 mg/day because it's the in-range dose closest to what he was taking, to help keep his nerve pain controlled.\n\nI didn't change his other medications (atorvastatin, metoprolol, amlodipine, nitroglycerin, acetaminophen) because the policy doesn't cover them.\n\nYour note is signed in his chart (doc-t0003). It lists the values, each rule's result and the changes, with the results and policy cited.\n\nStill to do: no one has told the patient or the pharmacy about the dose changes yet. The note also suggests watching for bleeding and for nerve pain control, and repeating creatinine and a measured weight at his next visit.",
